WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:03.680
Standalone movies are so 1980s.

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:07.860
These days, it's rare for a movie to end without
laying the groundwork for some sort of sequel

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:08.600
or franchise.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.180
Unfortunately, a lot can change between movies.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.389
Directors leave projects and long-term priorities
change, all of which can leave compelling

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:17.220
cliffhangers to dangle in the wind.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.820
From replacement spies vanishing between movies
to abandoned apocalyptic endings, here are

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.040
a few cliffhangers that movie sequels ignored.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.320
Aw, rats

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.000
The original Species, released in 1995, follows
a group of scientists trying to stop an alien-human

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.120
hybrid intent on breeding with humanity.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.400
While the scientists are able to ultimately
destroy the hybrid, the movie ends on a rat

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.000
that's exhibiting some strange behavior after
eating the corpse.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.120
Instead of following up on that plot thread,
Species II just doubles down on the creepy,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.710
erotic themes of the first movie, portraying
scientists trying to stop a brand new, mating-obsessed

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:52.840
alien-human hybrid.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.100
That hungry rat, and the implication that
the genetic mutation can be introduced through

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.820
food rather than intercourse, is totally ignored.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.520
We'll never know what we missed.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.160
"Oh!

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:03.700
He's making a pizza!"

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.140
That's just corny

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.020
What Children of the Corn started as a simple
Stephen King short story about a group of

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.110
kids in a midwestern town worshipping a demonic
entity, it's since spun into a massive franchise

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.040
with nearly ten movies and counting.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.720
Obviously, that required a bit of an escalation
from the original premise, which is what happens

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:28.150
in Children of the Corn III: Urban Harvest,
when two children of the corn are adopted

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.100
into foster care in Chicago.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:30.920
They bring corn.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.260
"That's nice lookin corn."

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.520
Through a series of violent events, one of
the children is able to summon the demonic

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.400
corn monster to Chicago while also distributing
mind-controlling corn all over the world.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.159
An entire world of mind-controlled children
isn't a bad hook to hang a horror film on.

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.001
Unfortunately, Children of the Corn 4: The
Gathering would return the series to its roots,

00:01:52.001 --> 00:01:56.261
as it were, by taking things back to the midwest,
ignoring the worldwide implications of the

00:01:56.261 --> 00:01:57.120
previous film.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.380
There was only one appropriate reaction to
the unrelated sequel.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.820
"Get that corn outta my face!"

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:05.580
A missed call

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.300
Like Children of the Corn, The Lawnmower Man
is so far removed from the original story

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.500
about a murderous, naked lawnmower man that
King successfully sued to remove his name

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.360
from the finished movie.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:19.920
The film follows Jobe, a greenskeeper who
becomes a vengeful, cyberspace god through

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.460
a series of science experiments.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.680
Halfway through the film, Jobe promises to
prove his god-like power by making every phone

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.040
in the world ring simultaneously once he's
achieved unlimited cybernetic control.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.240
An omnipotent, technology-controlling despot
seems like a great place to start a sequel,

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.780
but The Lawnmower Man 2: Beyond Cyberspace
features a largely new group of characters

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.300
in a cyberpunk Los Angeles.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.000
Jobe makes his return as an antagonist, but
using a different actor.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.950
His triumph in the first film is ignored in
favor of a retcon that left him crippled by

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.380
the climactic explosion in the first Lawnmower
Man.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:02:59.880
Bourne again

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.200
As one might guess given its title, the Bourne
franchise is focused on the adventures of

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.020
amnesiac assassin Jason Bourne.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.440
The formula worked so well that even when
Matt Damon took a hiatus from the series,

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.440
it moved on without him for 2012's The Bourne
Legacy.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.980
Following Jeremy Renner as Bourne's fellow
spy Aaron Cross, the film tried to capitalize

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.690
on the world-building of prior films with
a character similar to Jason Bourne and a

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.640
familiar story.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.080
Unfortunately for them, the movie premiered
to mixed reviews and an ambivalent audience.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.300
The filmmakers heard that message loud and
clear when they completely ignored the ending

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.129
of The Bourne Legacy, which featured Cross
wounded after a shootout and a close escape

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:35.629
on a boat.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.740
The next sequel, Jason Bourne, forgets about
any boat-related cliffhangers, putting the

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.620
focus squarely back on Damon's character.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.500
Dino dos and don'ts

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.290
It's fitting for a movie franchise about giant
prehistoric dinosaurs that each movie in the

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.440
Jurassic Park series tends to get bigger.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:55.360
Even once a balance seems to reached by Jurassic
Park 3 with the escaped dinosaurs settling

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.440
on an island, the film ends with Pteranodons
seeking out new nesting grounds.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.720
It's like Dr. Ian Malcolm always says:

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.880
"Life, uh, finds a way."

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:07.240
Unfortunately, the ending went too big.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:11.860
As cool as it would be to see dinosaurs out
in the world, 2012's Jurassic World decided

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:15.740
to skip the whole "dinosaurs terrorizing the
mainland" thing, and the fourth film just

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:19.840
returned to the original, fully-functional
Jurassic Park, instead of going bigger, giving

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.420
us very little reason to, well,

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:23.980
"Hold onto your butts."

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:26.860
Loss Leader

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.400
While it's been somewhat forgotten, 2008's
The Incredible Hulk is still an important

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.540
building block for modern-day Marvel films.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:37.740
While Ed Norton's version of Bruce Banner
wouldn't make the jump to later films, William

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.160
Hurt's "Thunderbolt" Ross would later return
to the MCU for Captain America: Civil War.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.860
Further connecting the movie to the larger
MCU is the ending sequence that features Robert

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.220
Downey Jr.'s Tony Stark dropping some foreshadowing
for the Avengers.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.900
"What if I told you we were putting a team
together?"

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:56.500
"Who's we?"

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.880
Despite all that, there's still at least one
cliffhanger that has never been resolved:

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:06.600
Samuel Sterns' irradiated transformation into
his big-headed comic book counterpart, the

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.100
Leader.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:12.440
"I am the most advanced mind the world has
ever seen!"

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.149
The supervillain is one of the Hulk's most
famous foes, so it makes sense that the filmmakers

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:19.240
would want to tease his possible appearance
in the sequel.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.680
Since his first appearance, a slew of different
Marvel movies have come out, and Hulk's nemesis

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.680
hasn't re-appeared on the big screen.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.180
Why?

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.220
Because Black Widow capped him off-screen,
in Marvel's official Avengers tie-in comics.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:34.819
To this day, he's just hanging out in a prison
somewhere, completely forgotten by the Marvel

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:35.320
Universe.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:36.160
What a way to go.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.180
"How dare you!"

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:40.620
Thanks for watching!

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.020
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.100
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

